# Git LFS, by FILE TYPE rather than by path.
#
# A path rule (`presentations/the-rectangle/audio/*.wav`) was the first
# version and it is a hand-maintained list wearing a smaller hat: the next
# talk in the next directory gets plain-git binaries and nobody notices until
# a clone is huge. The type is the durable fact — a wav is a wav wherever it
# lands.
#
# FORWARD-ONLY, AND THAT IS NOT A COMPROMISE. Adding a type here does not
# touch blobs already committed: the 21 GIFs and 9 PNGs in history stay plain
# git objects and keep checking out as real files, with or without git-lfs
# installed. Only the next write of a matching file becomes an LFS object.
# Verified rather than assumed — setting `*.gif` left `git status` clean
# across docs/media/demos/, which is what "existing blobs are untouched"
# actually looks like.
#
# So the payoff is prospective, and it is the real one. The docs-and-demos
# workflow re-records EVERY GIF in one go; at 7.3MB a sweep, each pass
# currently lands in history forever. That is precisely the cost LFS exists
# to remove, and it stops with the next sweep rather than needing a migrate.
#
# No history rewrite is planned. `git lfs migrate` on the existing GIFs would
# reclaim that 7.3MB, but it rewrites shared history and is a repo-wide call
# nobody has made.
#
# WHY THE AUDIO IS COMMITTED AT ALL. The Rectangle's 24 rendered takes look
# like derived output and mostly are, but every **DURATION:** marker in its
# NARRATION.md is measured with ffprobe against these exact files. A clone
# that re-renders instead of fetching gets a script whose timings describe
# audio it does not have. Piper is deterministic enough to sound the same and
# not enough to be the same file.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE:
#
#   The .txt receipts beside each wav. LFS is a fix for SIZE and for
#   BINARINESS, and 470 bytes of plain English is neither — all 24 together
#   are 20KB. Storing them here would cost the things that make them useful:
#   they would stop diffing in review and in `git log -p`, which is exactly
#   how you audit a script change, and they would stop being greppable. It
#   would also break the check they exist for, because present.py compares
#   each receipt to the current script to decide a take is stale, and against
#   a pointer file every take reads stale. Twenty kilobytes buys none of that
#   back.
#
#   voices/ — gitignored outright rather than stored. 224MB of piper's own
#   models, re-downloaded by say.sh on first run, and not ours to
#   redistribute.
#
# CONSEQUENCES FOR A CLONE WITHOUT GIT-LFS. Everything still works; some
# files are pointer text. The wavs re-render with `say.sh all` and only the
# exact timings are lost. GIFs recorded before this rule play as they always
# did; ones recorded after will not animate in `cmd/browser` until the
# objects are fetched. CI is unaffected either way — actions/checkout
# defaults to lfs:false, and nothing in the build or the test suite reads a
# committed GIF (cmd/browser's gifplay tests encode their own into a temp
# dir).
*.wav filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.gif filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.png filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
